etabolic syndrome: diabetes and systemic symptoms but no direct metabolic alarm features", "Orthopedic syndrome: foot fracture history but no current musculoskeletal findings", "Multiple myeloma: elevated calcium but lacks characteristic Bence-Jones proteinuria or lytic bone lesions in presented labs"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fatigue)", "Leukocytosis with normal differential", "Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1838238165475842, "posterior": 0.1838238165475842, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.5514714496427527, "posterior": 0.5514714496427527, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "urgency", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.14066713846616485, "posterior": 0.14066713846616485, "danger": 0.65, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.194, "danger": 0.45, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Other Myeloid Neoplasm with Unique Features", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.06, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute constitutional presentation with elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase favor chronic myeloid leukemia over polycythemia vera or myelofibrosis?", "summary": "Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAK) is a key differentiator favoring chronic myeloid leukemia over polycythemia vera/myelofibrosis, as LAK elevation is characteristic of CML but not typically seen in these other conditions."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal leukocyte differential with elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase argue against de novo AML in favor of a chronic myeloid process?", "summary": "A normal differential with elevated LAK strongly argues against de novo AML (B1), as AML typically shows blasts and abnormal cell morphology, while favoring chronic myeloid processes like CML (B2)."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the normal leukocyte differential and elevated alkaline phosphatase inconsistent with chronic lymphocytic leukemia or multiple myeloma?", "summary": "The normal differential and elevated LAK are inconsistent with chronic lymphocytic leukemia (B2's representative) and multiple myeloma (B3's representative), as these conditions do not elevate LAK. This weakens B3 but does not directly affect B2."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of fever/infection signs and presence of constitutional symptoms disfavor leukemoid reaction as the explanation for leukocytosis?", "summary": "The absence of infection signs and presence of constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fatigue) disfavor leukemoid reaction (B4), which typically occurs in response to infections or inflammation."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's lack of bone pain or serum creatinine elevation challenge the alternative myeloid neoplasm hypothesis?", "summary": "Normal creatinine and absence of bone pain reduce suspicion for aggressive myeloid neoplasms (B5), which often present with organ dysfunction or marrow infiltration symptoms."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "The strongest finding inconsistent with Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (B2) is the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAK), which is not typically seen in polycythemia vera or essential thrombocythemia, though it supports CML."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.495], ["B4", 0.234], ["B3", 0.133]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis (B2) posterior (0.495) < 0.75 and dangerous alternatives (B1: 0.0825 posterior, danger 0.85) remain unresolved. Tree depth (level 1) < max allowed (3). No info exhaustion due to unexplored discriminators like blood smear or genetic testing."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.85, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4954128440366974, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Myeloproliferative disorder', 'Multiple myeloma'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.495}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "posterior": 0.551, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B4", "B1", "B3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=40%, source=primekg]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm: bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
